{"id":"chromecore","title":"Chromecore","url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Chromecore","rev":224469,"fetched":"2026-10-16","lead":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Chromecore"]}," (also known as ",{"t":"b","c":["Y2K Chromecore"]},") is a design aesthetic that was popular from roughly 1999 to 2006, a subgenre of ",{"t":"a","c":["Y2K Futurism"],"id":"y2k-futurism"},". It focused on metallic-looking objects and products, most commonly CDs, DVDs, computers, video games, toys, phones, and digital cameras, having a sleek, chromatic gray texture. It was characterized by a distinct aesthetic period, encapsulating fashion, hardware design, and furnishings shining with technological optimism."]},{"t":"p","c":["It started as an branch of ",{"t":"a","c":["Y2K Futurism"],"id":"y2k-futurism"},", becoming a common color scheme on product design and advertisement in the early-mid 2000s, that was unique yet clean for its time. Most examples described as \"Chromecore\" are machinery and handheld gaming devices of the ",{"t":"a","c":["Y2K era"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Y2K"},", also overlapping somewhat with ",{"t":"a","c":["Frutiger Aero"],"id":"frutiger-aero"},". Its popularity declined in the late 2000s in favor of materials like anodized aluminum, glass, and carbon fiber, and a shift to more \"nature-oriented\" designs of Frutiger Aero. This decline happened due to oversaturation, short durability in some products, and lack of colorful and diverse design possibilities."]}],"sections":[{"title":"Visuals","id":"visuals","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["The visuals consists of shiny graphics and textures that have a metal chrome, bead blast or stainless steel finish to it. Within product design, the visuals are achieved with the use of silver plastic, that mimics the chrome finish. The silver and gray tones seen in the aesthetic are bright, reflective, clean and lustrous. Molded curves and shapes are also key features of this aesthetic too."]},{"t":"p","c":["Websites made using Geocities or Adobe Flash such as ",{"t":"a","c":["2Advanced Studios v3"],"href":"https://www.webdesignmuseum.org/gallery/2advanced-studios-v3-2001"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Space X"],"href":"https://www.webdesignmuseum.org/gallery/spacex-2002"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Loyal Digital studios"],"href":"https://www.webdesignmuseum.org/gallery/loyal-digital-studios-2001"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Design Insites"],"href":"https://www.webdesignmuseum.org/gallery/design-insites-2001"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["WK Website"],"href":"https://www.webdesignmuseum.org/gallery/wk-website-2003"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["RealityBid"],"href":"https://www.webdesignmuseum.org/gallery/realitybid-2002"},", and ",{"t":"a","c":["Taig Khris"],"href":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1MGmbRofaY"}," embraced this style, which used similar themes with a silver finish."]}]},{"title":"Technology","id":"technology","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Sony Aibo Ers-111 (1999)"]},{"c":["Kyocera VP-210 (1999)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Motorola C332"]}," (2002)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Motorola V70"]}," (2002)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Samsung SGH-T100"]}," (2002)"]},{"c":["Windows Server 2003 (2003)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Motorola C200"]}," (2003)"]},{"c":["Panasonic q (2003)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Samsung SGH-V200"]}," (2003)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Nokia N-Gage"]}," (2003)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Game Boy Advance SP"]}," (2003)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Nokia 6800 series"]}," (2003-2005)"]},{"c":["Sony Walkman D-ne1 (2003)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Qtek 9090"]}," (2004)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Samsung SGH-E300"]}," (2004)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Motorola RAZR V3"]}," (2004)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Nintendo DS [First Model]"]}," (2004/2005)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Game Boy Micro"]}," (2005)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Gizmondo"]}," (2005)"]},{"c":["Nokia 8800 (2005)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Nokia N70"]}," (2005)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Nokia E70"]}," (2006)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Motorola Aura"]}," (2008)"]},{"c":["Nokia 660